src=['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/bminor/glibc/master/sysdeps/unix/sysv/linux/x86_64/syscall.S','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/torvalds/linux/master/arch/x86/entry/entry_64.S','https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/vdso.7.html','https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/read.2.html']
for old,new in [('The previous lesson said user programs can\'t touch hardware.','Ordinary programs normally access protected devices through the kernel; authorised user-space drivers are an exception.'),('**Kernel mode**: everything is allowed: page tables, interrupt control, I/O ports, all memory.','**Kernel mode**: privileged operations are available, but mappings, memory protections, hardware rules and any hypervisor still constrain accesses.'),('**Exceptions** are *synchronous and unintentional*: the instruction just executed caused a problem.','In this introductory classification, **faults** are synchronous conditions caused by the current instruction. Architectural terminology also includes deliberate traps among exceptions.'),('  save user registers\n  switch to kernel stack\n  sys_call_table[rax] -> __x64_sys_write','  preserve user stack pointer\n  switch to kernel stack\n  save user registers\n  dispatch number -> __x64_sys_write'),('  sysret       -> back to ring 3','  sysret/iret   -> back to ring 3'),('You can bypass the libc wrapper and see the raw interface:','You can bypass the named syscall wrapper using libc\'s generic `syscall()` function. It still translates errors to `-1` and `errno`; this is not raw inline assembly:'),('A function call costs about a nanosecond. A syscall costs much more, for several reasons:','System calls add work beyond an ordinary function call; the size of that overhead depends on the operation and machine:'),('A trivial syscall like `getppid` typically costs on the order of **100 nanoseconds** on modern x86 hardware, and several hundred nanoseconds or more with mitigations. Exact figures vary a lot by CPU and kernel version, so measure on your own system.','> [!note] Content gap: syscall latency\n> No reproducible benchmark is supplied for the original nanosecond figures. Universal function-call, syscall and vDSO timing numbers are omitted; measure the chosen CPU, kernel, mitigations and workload.'),('At about 200 ns each','At an explicitly hypothetical 200 ns each'),('it costs tens of nanoseconds instead of hundreds.','it avoids kernel-entry overhead when that clock and clocksource support the fast path.'),('- A syscall costs roughly 100 ns or more, plus cache and TLB pollution; batching, buffering and `io_uring` amortise it.','- Syscall overhead depends on the implementation and workload; batching, buffering and `io_uring` can amortise entry overhead.'),('Traps are deliberate (syscalls), exceptions are errors in the current instruction (page faults), interrupts are external and asynchronous (the timer).','In the simplified terminology here, syscall traps are deliberate, faults are synchronous conditions such as page faults, and device interrupts are asynchronous. CPU manuals use more specific categories.')]:edit(a,old,new,'Correct ABI details and scope architectural terminology or unsupported timing claims.',src)
qe(b,'proc-syscalls-q3','prompt','A glibc C program calls `printf("x\\n")` 1,000 times with stdout redirected to a regular file. Assume default full buffering with a buffer of at least 2,000 bytes, successful complete writes and normal exit. Roughly how many `write` syscalls carry that output?','Supply buffering and successful-write assumptions needed for exact count.',src)
qe(b,'proc-syscalls-q3','explanation','An unbuffered stream can cause much smaller writes, including single-character writes; there is no universal prohibition. The stated fully buffered setup combines output.','printf may issue single-character writes in other configurations.',src,option=2)
qe(b,'proc-syscalls-q5','text','About 261 ms','Align rounded answer with 261.12ms calculation.',src,option=1)
qe(b,'proc-syscalls-q5','explanation','The 1 MiB case alone is about 1.024 ms for 2,048 calls, not 0.5 ms; the saving is about 261.12 ms.','Correct distractor arithmetic.',src,option=0)
