qe(b,q['id'],'workedExample',q['workedExample'].replace('`getpid` enters and leaves the kernel in the *same* process; no other process runs. Only a blocking call like `read` on an empty pipe leads to a context switch.','`getpid` does not inherently require another process to run. Blocking, preemption or other scheduling decisions can cause a context switch, including during a nonblocking syscall.'),'Preemption can occur during nonblocking system calls.',src)
qe(b,'proc-syscalls-q7','workedExample','strace observes kernel entries, so calls completed by a supported vDSO fast path are absent. On x86-64 the vDSO exposes time-related functions and getcpu; exact support depends on the requested clock and clocksource. A fallback can occur for unsupported clock IDs or clocksources, so trace and inspect the actual environment rather than assuming a particular nanosecond cost.','Remove unmeasured vDSO latency and single-cause diagnosis.',src)
qe(b,'proc-syscalls-q8','prompt','A program reads a framed TCP byte stream with `n = read(fd, buf, 65536);`. It returns 1,200. The code assumes it has the whole 65,536-byte frame. What is wrong?','Datagram sockets have different truncation semantics; scope to TCP.',src)
qe(b,'proc-syscalls-q9','workedExample','On x86-64, SYSCALL stores the return instruction pointer in rcx and flags in r11, so Linux uses r10 for argument four. A named four-argument wrapper can move rcx to r10. The generic glibc syscall(number, ...) wrapper has an extra leading argument: it moves r8 to r10 while shifting the other arguments. SYSRET uses rcx/r11 on a suitable return path; Linux can also use IRET.','Generic glibc syscall() shifts its extra syscall-number argument; original rcx->r10 claim was wrong.',src)
qe(b,'proc-syscalls-q10','workedExample','At a baseline 400,000 operations/s on one saturated core, time per operation is 2.5 microseconds. An assumed extra 0.5 microseconds would raise it to 3.0 microseconds: about 333,333 operations/s, a 16.7% throughput drop, rather than 20%. A 20% throughput drop would correspond to 3.125 microseconds per operation in this simple model. Batch calls or buffer output, then measure; the prompt alone does not isolate every cost.','Distinguish extra CPU demand at fixed rate from saturated throughput reduction.',src)
